      "title": "Advanced Skills through Multiple Adversarial Motion Priors in Reinforcement Learning",
      "takeaway": "This work addresses the difficulty of reward tuning in reinforcement learning for locomotion by extending adversarial motion priors so that a policy can learn multiple, discretely switchable motion styles, including styles combined with motion-data-free skills. The authors report real-world experiments on a wheeled-legged robot covering skills learned from existing RL controllers and trajectory optimization, such as ducking and walking, plus a novel transition between quadrupedal and humanoid configurations that requires standing up, navigating on two wheels, and sitting down. They also report that reverse playback of the stand-up movement helped the robot discover feasible sit-down behavior without manual reward tuning. When reading, inspect how style switching is represented and how the multiple priors are combined during training.",
